entries.body:
Protocol designed by Tadge Dryja to compact the UTXO set of Bitcoin nodes using an accumulator built on Merkle trees. Unlike the classic UTXO set, which requires significant storage space, Utreexo drastically reduces the required memory by storing only the roots of the Merkle trees. This allows the node to verify the existence of the UTXOs used as transaction inputs, without having to keep the complete set of UTXOs. By using Utreexo, each node keeps only cryptographic fingerprints called Merkle roots. When a transaction is made, the user provides the inclusion proofs of the UTXOs and the corresponding Merkle paths. The node can thus verify transactions without storing the entire UTXO set. Let's take an example with a diagram to understand this mechanism:

![Utreexo inclusion proof: with UTXO 3, HASH 4 and HASH 1-2, the node reconstructs the Merkle root that it stores in RAM.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/u/utreexo/assets/image-1.png)

In this example, I intentionally reduced the UTXO set to 4 UTXOs to make it easier to understand. In reality, you have to imagine that there are almost 140 million UTXOs on Bitcoin as I write these lines. In this diagram, the Utreexo node would only have to keep the Merkle root in RAM. If it receives a transaction spending UTXO no. 3 (in black), the proof would consist of the following elements:
* UTXO 3;
* HASH 4;
* HASH 1-2.

Thanks to this information transmitted by the sender of the transaction, the Utreexo node performs the following verifications:
* It calculates the hash of UTXO 3, which gives it HASH 3;
* It concatenates HASH 3 with HASH 4;
* It calculates their hash, which gives it HASH 3-4;
* It concatenates HASH 3-4 with HASH 1-2;
* It calculates their hash, which gives it the Merkle root.

If the Merkle root it obtains through its process is the same as the Merkle root it stored in its RAM, then it is convinced that UTXO no. 3 is indeed part of the UTXO set.

This method reduces RAM requirements for full node operators. However, Utreexo has limitations, notably the increase in block size due to the additional proofs and the potential dependence of Utreexo nodes on *Bridge Nodes* to obtain the missing proofs. *Bridge Nodes* are traditional full nodes that provide the necessary proofs to Utreexo nodes, thus allowing complete verification. This approach offers a compromise between efficiency and decentralization, making transaction validation more accessible to users with limited resources.
